re crowded projects like the river  park farm could be a model for future  urban spaces  now to a crisis gripping large parts of  sudan  weeks of heavy rains in the north  african country have triggered severe  flooding and nearly 600 thousand people  have been affected the area around the  capital khartoum has been hit hard  especially rural villages and towns that  have been cut off from aid sudan's flood  task force is doing its best to help but  they face an uphill battle many roads  are submerged and there are growing  concerns that mosquitoes and tainted  drinking water could spread disease  throughout the region  nbc reporter mohammad osman went to the  flood-hit state of white nile and sent  us this report  we've traveled about 300 kilometers  south from the sudanese capital cartoon  into the heart of the white nile state  heavy rainfall and flash flooding have  destroyed or damaged many towns and  villages making orientation difficult  you need a lot of patience and a guide  to help you find your way around  the flooding damaged homes and displaced  families throughout the region  flood waters have undermined the  foundations of this school making it  dangerous for children to attend class  the teachers have been forced to start  holding lectures in the schoolyard to  keep the children safe  six classrooms have been completely  ruined and we dare not go into them  we prefer to stay outside  four classrooms are still okay  how much damage is there  you can find damage throughout this  region  the school the health center all of the  houses that you see here everything is  submerged  there's water surrounding the entire  village  and the sky is still overcast  flood flood waters inundated this  village the environmental and health  risks are huge  fresh water is hard to come by and pools  of stagnant water provide ideal breeding  grounds for disease-carrying mosquitoes  survival has become very difficult  local people try to carry on as best  they can  but a 