ountry's oldest vineyard  which was founded in the 16th century  here in this cellar with over 1200  square meters of floor space we have 300  000 liters of wine  we also have a vintage collection  featuring over 7 000 bottles where the  oldest one is from 1949. we take  winemaking seriously in this region  in contrast to canada v vinogradi's  traditional charm the vina bellia is one  of the world's most modern wineries  construction finished on the massive  facility located near bonovo bordeaux  last year  we need to start processing as soon as  possible since we harvest manually  throughout the 583 hectares so the  grapes are brought here these tanks can  hold 8 million liters  wine making may be big business for some  but many families are rediscovering the  art of hawaiian making at home like here  in sousa  i've taken this over from my father who  was a good winemaker he sold his wine at  the end and on the doorstep like this  and we're continuing this tradition  near souza is mayavats where surduce or  narrow canyon-like roads are a popular  destination for tourists  and wine lovers on the hunt for  something out of the ordinary would do  well with a visit to erdut to see a  winemaking piece of art  here we have a barrel made of slavonian  oak with a capacity of 75 000 liters  it was made in 1990 by d ik giordinovac  and it's still in use today  throughout the danube basin  many small villages are expanding their  offerings for tourists  in addition to good wine we also offer  accommodation we have 10 double rooms  from roman times through the middle ages  and up to the present day croatia's  podunavia wine region has preserved the  tradition of handcrafted wine on the  banks of the danube  onwards now to bangladesh to a region  called movibazar with its warm climate  and rich soil the area exports fruits  and teas and bamboo it's a popular  building material in bangladesh because  it's both sturdy and sleek and it's used  in all sorts of handicrafts in our next  story etv's sakawat liton introduces us  to a man who's dedicated his life to  making bamboo furniture his products  have grown so popular they're in demand  around the country here's more  bamboo grows widely in bangladesh  and it's used for many different  purposes in rural regions from thatching  roofs to making baskets  amir hosain siraj decided to start  making bamboo furniture using  traditional techniques and it's become  his passion  his business has really taken off the  furniture is proving popular in his  native mulivi bazaar region and in other  parts of bangladesh as well  i mean  i wanted to do something different  in 2001 i started making furniture for  my apartment  a few people saw the furniture and began  to ask me if i would make them  so i turned it into a business  worked in a plant nursery  then he started making bamboo furniture  based on his own designs  then he started supplying furniture for  weddings and other social occasions  he has really expanded his contacts  district adorned with decorative bamboo  objects as well as furniture he's made  we are very proud of him  he has set up a new kind of craft  business  he is using bamboo to manufacture  furniture it's helping the development  of our country  right from the outset people were  attracted to the furniture amir made  this encouraged him to make the leap to  manufacturing it commercially  next door to the store is the workshop  where the furniture is made  siraj makes beds sofas chairs dining  tables and side tables trays and various  decorative objects  we place the bamboo rods in a water tank  for 72 hours and leave it to dry in the  shade for 15 days  after that it's ready to use  the bamboo furniture isn't just  attractive it's also durable  many bangladeshis are choosing to  furnish their homes with it  it's a growing interior design trend  there's a big demand for this type of  furniture  bangladeshis living abroad have told me  that it would sell in indonesia or  malaysia  i hope our country can export it  by using bamboo for the manufacturer  furniture we can reduce the pressure on  our precious wood supplies  bamboo is readily available in our  country if the government takes the  initiative bamboo furniture will become  popular in the entire country  amir siraj has strived to improve the  quality of his bamboo products it was a  long process but he's convinced he's  found the right technique to allow him  to offer his customers high quality and  sturdy handmade furniture  cane furniture is usually guaranteed to  last up to 20 years  but we have a 40-year guarantee  mr  didn't have the opportunity to publicize  his products via the mass media  despite that people come to him and  order furniture  and then get back in touch later  if the  both environmentally friendly and  aesthetically pleasing amer hosain  siraj's bamboo furniture is a  sustainable choice for interior  decoration  and finally to a culinary delicacy  that's stirring up controversy in china  it all centers around a rare caterpillar  fungus that's a highly potent substance  in chinese medicine it's believed to be  a cure for many diseases  it may not look like much a small brown  twig with a dried worm on the end but  the fungus is in such high demand with  both traditional chinese doctors and  cooks alike that it's worth more than  gold reporter anya koch explains  the tibetan plateau at 3 000 meters  above sea level is home to a fungus  that's taking the world by storm  in tibetan it's known as the yatsa yards  which means summer grass winter worm but  it's actually a fungus that invades the  caterpillar of the ghost moth and  digests it from within  in the winter it buries itself in the  ground and in spring it will grow these  grass-like parts because it's been  affected by the fungus  when peasants dig it they look for the  grassy part  the worm is already dead because the  fungus has killed it  the fungus grows out of the head of the  caterpillar host looking like a brown  twig emerging from the ground harvesting  it takes a keen eye  the dried fruiting bodies are then  bundled together and sold  we separate the damaged parts because  the broken ones are cheaper the whole  winds fetch higher prices  a kilo of caterpillar fungus costs  anywhere from five to nearly 45 000  euros making it many times more  expensive than the truffle  while the caterpillar fungus may not  look very appetizing it's in great  demand  a lot of my friends like to eat  caterpillar fungus  they usually give it to their parents  their elders and also to the children  but what makes the fungus so popular  pan chaoshi is an expert in traditional  chinese medicine he says the fungus is a  remedy for a variety of ailments  it's good for sexual dysfunctions and  for coughing up blood  the fungi are also a popular ingredient  in chinese cooking  served with beef tongue it's a delicacy  that's highly prized by wealthy chinese  serving caterpillar fungi is also a  status symbol among urban chinese  others are stockpiling the fungus as an  investment after all it's more expensive  now than gold  for many local families the fungus  harvest means welcome extra income but  as the fungus's value grows so do the  number of disputes that end in violence  and some professional fungi gatherers  have given up the harvest because of the  damage that's being done to the  environment  the pollution is very serious  now in my hometown the water is almost  dried up  the grass is dying also dry  this is very bad for us in the tibetan  areas and our lama our living buddha  told us never to dig out the fungus even  if we have nothing to eat for three days  in a row and we're starving  has found a new job  but as long as prices for the  caterpillar fungus soar  the harvest will continue  sounds delicious well that's all for  this week's edition of world stories for  all of us here and our partner stations  around the world we want to thank you  for watching you can see this episode  again and many more at our website  that's dw.de  world stories before you go we'll leave  you with this week's picks of the world  enjoy and see you next time  you

